  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 PM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nA few showers linger mainly across the inland Coastal Plains and \noffshore waters. Our sounding this morning at Corpus Christi \nmeasured a PWAT value of 2.07\", near the daily max (2.24\"), an \nextremely saturated profile. Intermittent light rain and/or \ndrizzle continues across the Coastal Bend due to weak surface \nconvergence. There isn't much mid- level support as this morning's\nshortwave moves northeast, bringing a lull in rain chances \ntonight. Another shortwave disturbance will move across the area \ntomorrow (Wednesday), bringing a low to medium chance of showers \nand thunderstorms over the Coastal Plains, with greatest chances \nover the Victoria Crossroads. \n\nRemnants of current Pacific Tropical Storm Vance will move into \nMexico then into South Texas Thursday, focusing the moisture axis \ncloser to the Rio Grande, shifting medium rain chances (30-60%) with \nit. The remnants will continue to shift northeastward, reducing rain \nchances to low (~20%) on Friday. A weak shortwave on Saturday could \nextend into the Victoria Crossroads and pose a low chance for \nshowers and thunderstorms, but overall flow becomes zonal by \nSaturday night, leading to near zero rain chances for trick or \ntreating.\n\nRain-free and slightly warmer conditions are expected Sunday and \nMonday under a ridge, with highs reaching the low 90s over the Brush \nCountry. A very amplified mid-level trough or closed low will move \ninto the Central Great Plains Tuesday, bringing a cold front across \nSouth Texas. Model guidance is in fairly good agreement with FROPA \nwith 6-hours of each other. We'll need to keep a close eye on this \nsynoptic flow pattern as it has the potential to bring a strong line \nof storms across parts of Texas, how far south it progresses remains \nhighly uncertain.\n\nLastly, minor coastal flooding remains likely during high tide in \nthe late afternoon and night hours with water reaching the dunes. In \naddition, there is an increased risk of rip currents. These \nhazardous beach conditions are caused by increased swell periods and \nnear astronomical high tide.",
